<p>Organic cow manure from India goes to two buyer types that make up almost all of this trade. Horticulture and garden-retail businesses in Europe and North America want a natural soil-improvement input, usually in retail-ready bags. Agricultural operations in South Asia, East Africa and Southeast Asia want bulk organic matter to build up soil that's naturally low in organic carbon. If you're reading this as either type of buyer, the sourcing conversation is largely the same — the packing conversation is not. This guide covers the forms and specification to order, the import-permit question that comes before everything else, how the price is built, and what goes wrong on a first shipment.</p>

<!-- slot:facts -->

<p>Two things are worth flagging honestly about the export data above. HS 3101 covers "animal or vegetable fertilizers" broadly, not cow manure specifically, so the figures include other products in the same customs heading. And India exported to 99 countries/regions under that heading in 2023, so the ranking reflects the largest buyers, not the only buyers. If your country isn't among them, that doesn't mean the trade doesn't happen — it means the shipments are smaller or less frequent, which is common for a specialty soil-improvement product moving in container-load or part-container quantities rather than bulk commodity volumes.</p>

<!-- insight:buyer-segmentation -->
