<p>Nepal and Sri Lanka took about 41% of India's tonnage under the heading at well under USD 0.40 per kg: bulk organic matter for fields, moved by road or short sea. At the other end, the United States, Bangladesh and Indonesia paid USD 3.70-5.50 per kg on a few hundred tonnes or less each, about 10 to 18 times the bulk price. Because HS 3101 also covers other animal and vegetable fertilizers, those high-value rows are likely to include processed or retail-packed products, not just bagged cow manure, so treat them as a different market rather than a price to aim for. Kenya, the Philippines and China sit between the two.</p>
<p>Scenario: a Kenyan plantation buying bulk granules in jumbo bags and a US garden-centre chain buying 5kg retail bags with its own artwork both appear under HS 3101. The plantation's offer is built on processing, moisture and freight; the retail buyer's adds bagging, printing and labelling, and is compared with other branded soil products, not with the Nepal average.</p>
