<h2>Which form to order</h2>
<p>Composted, sun-dried cow manure ships as powder, granules or cakes/pellets. Powder and granules dominate export volume because they're easier to handle, spread with standard equipment, and store without degrading. Cakes are a smaller, more traditional-use request. The form you choose affects both the container loading calculation and the price — finer processing costs more.</p>
<p>When we use "organic manure" or "organic fertilizer," we're describing the product type — animal-origin, non-synthetic. If your market has a specific labelling requirement attached to that word, share your compliance checklist before you order and we will confirm the documentation for your shipment.</p>
<!-- insight:spec-to-application -->
<h2>What NPK, organic carbon and moisture should you specify?</h2>
<p>The specification points to settle on any order:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Form</strong> — powder, granule or cake</li>
  <li><strong>Moisture maximum</strong> — 15-25% is typical; lower moisture means more usable material per shipped tonne</li>
  <li><strong>Organic carbon</strong> — 12-18% is typical</li>
  <li><strong>NPK range</strong> — N 0.5-1.5%, P 0.4-0.8%, K 0.5-1.2%; natural-material variation, confirmable via certificate of analysis per lot</li>
  <li><strong>Screening mesh</strong> — 2mm, 4mm or your own requirement, to remove stones and debris</li>
  <li><strong>Packing</strong> — bulk jumbo bags for agricultural use, or retail bags with your artwork for garden-centre programmes</li>
</ul>
<p>The numbers show what the product is for. One tonne at 1% nitrogen carries about 10 kg of N, roughly what 22 kg of urea (46% N) supplies. The same tonne at 12-18% organic carbon carries 120-180 kg of carbon, and that is what a soil-building buyer is paying for. So judge a lot on organic carbon, moisture and screening first, and treat NPK as a check that the material is what it claims to be, not as its main value. Many compost standards also look at the carbon-to-nitrogen (C:N) ratio as a sign of how fully the material has broken down: a lot at 15% organic carbon and 1% N has a C:N of 15:1. If your market's rules set a C:N limit, ask for it on the certificate of analysis.</p>
<p>Moisture changes how much of that you get. A tonne at 25% moisture holds about 750 kg of dry material; at 15% it holds about 850 kg, roughly 13% more for the same freight. A horticulture buyer blending into potting mixes usually wants fine powder at the lower end of the moisture range; a plantation spreading by machine cares more about consistent granule size.</p>
<!-- /insight -->

