<h2>What actually determines the price you're quoted</h2>
<p>There's no single per-tonne price for this product because it isn't a single product — powder, granules and cake have different processing costs, and moisture content changes how much usable material you're actually buying per shipped tonne. What moves the number: processing method and finished moisture level (drier, finer-screened material costs more to produce but ships more efficiently), packaging format (retail bags with custom artwork cost more per tonne than bulk jumbo bags), destination freight (this is a genuinely global product, so freight cost varies hugely by route — Nepal by land/short sea routing is a completely different economics than the Philippines or Kenya by container), and seasonal raw-material supply from dairy-belt collection regions.</p>
<p>Quotes are issued FOB, CIF or CFR against your specific form, moisture spec, packaging and destination port. To get an accurate quote fast, tell us: form (powder/granule/cake), moisture ceiling if you have one, packaging (bulk or retail bags, with or without your artwork), quantity, and destination port. That's what we need to come back with FOB/CIF pricing and a lead time.</p>
<p>Freight is the line that moves most with moisture, because cow manure is a low-value cargo and freight is charged on every tonne shipped, water included. On every 20 tonnes shipped, material at 25% moisture carries 5 tonnes of water; at 15% it carries 3. Scenario: two offers for the same destination differ only in moisture, 25% against 15%. The drier lot costs more per tonne to produce, but it delivers about 13% more dry material per container, and on a long route the freight saving can outweigh the processing premium. Compare offers per tonne of dry material, not per shipped tonne.</p>
