<h2>What goes wrong on a first order, and how to avoid it</h2>
<p>The most common dispute on this product is moisture content on arrival, not at loading — cow manure is hygroscopic and, unlike a sealed food product, buyers sometimes store bags in humid or uncovered conditions after delivery and then attribute the resulting weight/quality change to the shipment itself. We recommend agreeing a moisture ceiling in writing and specifying storage conditions on your end (dry, covered, off damp ground) as part of the order, since a contractually-compliant shipment can still degrade if stored wrong after arrival.</p>
<p>The second common issue is odor complaints at destination customs or in retail-adjacent storage. Properly composted and sun-dried manure has a mild, earthy smell, not a strong ammonia odor — a strong odor on arrival usually indicates the material wasn't fully composted before shipment or picked up moisture in transit. We compost and sun-dry fully before bagging specifically to avoid this, and it's a fair question to ask any supplier directly: how long was this composted, and to what moisture level before bagging?</p>
<p>Third, granule-size consistency matters more than buyers expect for equipment-based spreading (as opposed to hand application) — inconsistent granule size clogs mechanical spreaders and creates uneven application. Specify your screening mesh if you're supplying commercial growers using spreading equipment.</p>
<p>What first-time buyers most often get wrong: assuming the import-permit process is the same everywhere because "it's just manure." It isn't — some countries treat animal-origin fertilizer almost like a live-animal-product import (extensive permitting, treatment declarations, sometimes pre-shipment inspection requirements), while others treat it more like any other agricultural input. Check your specific country's requirement before you assume a timeline, not after you've already committed to a delivery date with your own customer.</p>
